  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef has an integer array AAA of length NNN such that 1≤Ai≤31 \\le A_i \\le 31≤Ai​≤3.\n\nChef is wondering how many pairs exist in his array, which have a prime sum. Formally, find the number of pairs (i,j)(i, j)(i,j) such that\n\n\n1≤i&lt;j≤N1 \\le i &lt; j \\le N1≤i&lt;j≤N\nAi+AjA_i + A_jAi​+Aj​ is prime.\nXXX is a prime integer if it is not 111, and has no other divisor except 111 and XXX. For example, 222, 131313 and 979797 are prime integers, while 111 and 444 are not.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN.\nThe second line contains NNN integers - A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the number of pairs with prime sums.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\le T \\le 1001≤T≤100",
      "1≤N≤1001 \\le N \\le 1001≤N≤100",
      "1≤Ai≤31 \\le A_i \\le 31≤Ai​≤3"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n2\n1 1\n3\n2 1 3",
        "output": "1\n2",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1 : The only pair is (1,2)(1, 2)(1,2) which has a sum of 222.\nTest Case 2 : The valid pairs are (1,2)(1, 2)(1,2) and (1,3)(1, 3)(1,3), having sums of 333 and 555 respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
